## Phase 6 Handoff

### 1. Tech debt incurred

- The plan's literal instruction — exercise the corpus from "`data-firebase`'s
  `androidUnitTest` suite" — could not be honored as written. Spiked it first rather
  than assuming: added Robolectric to `data-firebase`, and connecting GitLive's Android
  Firestore client to a live emulator from a JVM `androidUnitTest` failed three times in
  a row, each fix uncovering the next wall — (1) `Firebase.initialize(app)` needs
  `google-services.json`-generated resources that don't exist for a bare unit-test
  manifest; (2) fixed via explicit `FirebaseOptions`, then `Firebase.firestore` threw
  `"Firestore component is not present"` until `testOptions.unitTests
  .isIncludeAndroidResources = true` was added so AGP merges dependency manifests; (3)
  fixed that too, then `FirebaseApp.initializeApp()` itself threw
  `IllegalStateException: The Crashlytics build ID is missing` — Firebase eagerly
  initializes every registered product on init, `shared/build.gradle.kts:190` declares
  `firebase-crashlytics` as a dependency, `data-firebase` depends on `shared`, and only
  `app/build.gradle.kts` applies the Crashlytics Gradle plugin that generates the build
  ID resource. This is also why every existing `GitLive*CacheManagerTest` in this module
  already documents Firestore reads as "outside a JVM unit test's reach" — the spike
  confirmed that documented limitation is real, not stale caution.

### 2. How it was resolved (immediately, not deferred)

- Reverted the Robolectric spike in full (`data-firebase/build.gradle.kts`, the deleted
  `FirestoreEmulatorSpikeTest.kt`) — confirmed via `git diff --stat data-firebase/` is
  empty.
- Pivoted to the corpus test's actual goal — catching a field/path renamed on one side
  and not the other — without needing a live Kotlin reader: `firestore-tests/fixtures/
  shared-corpus.json` is the one JSON fixture both real readers assert against.
  `firestore-tests/seed-shared-corpus.mjs` seeds it into the emulator (security rules
  disabled for the write, matching how content ingestion actually reaches Firestore —
  scripts/Cloud Functions, never a client). `firestore-tests/sharedCorpus.contract
  .test.mjs` reads it back and asserts the exact field names data-firebase's
  `GitLive*CacheManager` `@Serializable` DTOs decode (`OirBatchDocDto`,
  `WATWordBatchDocDto`, etc.) — a Node proxy for the Kotlin reader, not the reader
  itself, and its docstring says so explicitly rather than implying real Kotlin
  coverage. `web/tests/unit/contracts/sharedCorpusEmulator.test.ts` reads the same
  fixture through the real `firebase/firestore` SDK (`connectFirestoreEmulator`) and the
  real `ContentRepository`, not a mock — closing §3.6's actual gap (a per-platform mock
  can lock in a wrong path; a real read against a shared fixture cannot).
- Deleted `web/tests/unit/contracts/firestoreSchemaContract.test.ts` — a mocked test
  asserting `study_materials` path/mapping via `vi.mock('firebase/firestore', ...)`, now
  fully superseded by the real corpus test's `getStudyMaterials` assertion.
- Narrowed (did not delete) `testContentSchemaContract.test.ts` — its path-literal
  assertions are superseded by the corpus test, but its business-logic assertions
  (TAT blank-card append, OIR anti-cheat stripping, `readTime` string parsing) have no
  duplicate anywhere else, so deleting the whole file would have been a real coverage
  loss for logic the corpus test doesn't re-assert per field. Added a docstring
  explaining the narrowed scope so a future reader doesn't mistake it for the
  drift-detection layer again.
- Wired `.github/workflows/main-ci.yml`'s `firestore-rules` job to install web's deps,
  seed the corpus, and run both real readers in one `firebase emulators:exec
  --only firestore,auth` invocation, against the default project (`.firebaserc`'s
  `ssbmax-49e68`) — matching `web/.env`'s `VITE_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID`, which is why this
  runs as a separate `emulators:exec` call from the existing rules-unit-test step
  (that one deliberately uses a different demo project).

